For more up-to-date information, please check the web-site:
http://Lyntin.sourceforge.net/



* NOTES

This entire document is written off the top of my head and the contents
of it are likely to change before 3.0 actually gets released.  It's
possible this document will at some point get merged with the ARCHITECTURE
document.



* SYNOPSIS

Lyntin was built for the hacker/mudder.  This is a special breed of
person who is mudding and has a series of complex problems they want
to solve and mere aliases and triggers just aren't going to do the
trick.

Tintin++ has a series of commands that almost but not quite implements
(ed. bad word choice) a programming language.  Building "macros" in 
this way is complex and solves a small set of problems, but the more 
complex problems really need a full language and a library to solve.  
As such, Lyntin was built with Python and allows hackers to use Python 
to enhance Lyntin.

Because Lyntin is so inextricably intertwined with Python, you can
extend Lyntin to do anything without going through hoops: check your
email, record URLs you see and open up a browser to visit them, export
mudmail, building complex bots....

There a a few different methods of hacking Lyntin to suit your needs.  
They are briefly outlined here and described in more detail in their 
respective sections:

	1. editing the source code
	2. building modules
	3. command line

Before you continue in this document, you should read through the
ARCHITECTURE guide which walks you through how Lyntin is architected
from a bird's eye view.  Understanding that will make this document
much more interesting and more clear.



* EDITING THE SOURCE CODE

Lyntin is entirely written in Python.  The source code is well organized
(as of 3.0) and documentation exists on the web-site as well as in the 
source code distribution on how Lyntin is architected and details on
the internals.

Lyntin is distributed under the GPL.  You should read this prior to
making changes to the source code and distributing them so you don't
accidentally find yourself in violation.

If you patch Lyntin, we'd love to see your work!  Send it to the
Lyntin developers list and depending on what the patch does, it may
get incorporated into the Lyntin base code.



* BUILDING MODULES

FIXME - This is likely to change in the near future prior to the final
3.0 release.

Lyntin has a modules package and all the commands are implemented in
the basic and advanced modules.  You can add modules at your whim.
The purpose of Lyntin modules is to centralize your efforts into one
file that you have control over.  This enables you to have one file
you can drop into a Lyntin installation and it works without editing
configuration files or installation files.

This is cool because then your module is easy to install should you
upgrade your version of Lyntin.  And it's easy to distribute too because
it's one file.



* COMMAND LINE

Lyntin allows you to execute arbitrary Python at the command line.
The code will be executed in some module--(to be determined).

For example:

^<<
	#@print "hello"
^>>

will print hello to the console.  There are some significant differences
between Lyntin 2.x and 3.x in how this works.  In the 2.x series, 
raw Python was executed in the user.py module.  It was encouraged that
you add your own functions to the user.py module and then these would
be available to you at the Lyntin command line.

In 3.0 user.py is a module in the modules directory.  It does not come
with Lyntin--it's something you'll have to create.  This is a little
weird in that you have to go write your own user.py module, but on the
flip side, it helps when you upgrade Lyntin--you won't accidentally
overwrite your user.py Lyntin module because it doesn't come with
Lyntin.

If you don't have a user.py module, raw Python code gets executed in 
the advanced.py module where the #@ functionality exists.

